    "narrative": "For the July 21st slate, our offensive core is headlined by **Bryce Harper** (70.17) and **Luis Arraez** (66.78). **Bryce Harper** finds himself in a prime spot against Justin Wrobleski; the favorable wind conditions and the high-leverage order position drive his elite rating. Meanwhile, **Luis Arraez** benefits from an outstanding matchup against Luinder Avila. With an xERA nearing 5.00, Avila provides the perfect environment for **Luis Arraez** to maximize his high-contact floor, while **Jung Hoo Lee** (63.63) also thrives in this same matchup, remaining a high-value start despite a modest penalty for the opposing battery\u2019s efficiency at holding runners. **Taylor Ward** (66.63) and **Gleyber Torres** (64.55) round out our most potent offensive threats, both bolstered by favorable platoon advantages, though **Gleyber Torres** carries a 41% rain risk that requires monitoring.\n\nThe most difficult decision on today's lineup card was excluding **Dylan Beavers** (50.69) and **Geraldo Perdomo** (50.44). **Dylan Beavers** was the primary candidate to displace **Jeremiah Jackson** (47.43), but the platoon disadvantage against the left-handed Eduardo Rivera ultimately capped his upside. Similarly, while **Geraldo Perdomo** offers the stolen base profile we covet, the matchup against the stingy Jack Perkins suppressed his efficiency score just enough to keep him on the pine. We are prioritizing the higher ceiling of **Jonathan Aranda** (61.34) today, as his platoon edge against Kevin Gausman keeps him ahead of the bench options despite the quality of the opposing arm. Managers should keep a close eye on the official lineups, as our projections for **Cal Raleigh**, **Yainer Diaz**, and the rest of the squad are currently predicated on historical order assumptions; any drop in the batting order for the likes of **Daulton Varsho** (50.95) or **Drew Gilbert** (53.48) could further compress their marginal scores.",
